course: dictionary
lesson: 12634
title: Disparage
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: To disparage someone is to speak about them as if they do not matter or are not any good. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, news and exams. The clue is that the speaker is putting someone down.
explain: «The coach did not disparage the team's efforts; she praised how hard they tried.» The word not tells you the coach was kind. «Her friend began to disparage her drawing, saying it looked like a sack of potatoes.» The unkind comment is the clue: disparage means to put someone down.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «The critic loved to disparage new films.» Does put down make sense here? Yes, so your idea is right. Disparage is close to belittle: both make someone or something seem small.
fact: Disparage is a verb: I disparage, he disparages, they disparaged, she was disparaging.
fact: «Disparage» means to speak about someone or something as if they are not important or not good.
fact: «Disparaging remarks» means unkind words that put someone down.
fact: «Not to disparage» is a polite phrase used before a comment that might sound rude, such as «not to disparage your efforts, but this could be neater».
exercise: «She did not want to disparage his efforts.» What does disparage mean here? | *To put his efforts down, as if they were not worth much | To hide his efforts away | To praise his efforts very loudly | To copy his efforts exactly
exercise: «The article tried to disparage the new park.» What is the writer doing? | Telling everyone the park is wonderful | *Making the park seem small or not good | Counting the trees in the park | Drawing a map of the park
exercise: Which word means the same as disparage? | *Belittle | Admire | Forgive | Celebrate
test: Ella said, «Not to disparage your poem, but it is far too short.» Why does Ella start with not to disparage? | To say the poem is perfect | *To soften a comment that might sound unkind | To tell the poet to stop writing | To shout at the poet
test: Which one is an example of disparaging someone's work? | Saying «I like the way you did the sky» | *Saying «Your painting is hopeless, you should give up» | Saying «Can you show me how you painted it?» | Saying «Your painting has lovely colours»
exam: «The head teacher warned pupils not to disparage each other's work.» What should pupils avoid? | Admiring their classmates' ideas | *Putting down their classmates' work | Asking each other for help | Sharing their books
